  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts the evolution of laboratory automation from classic bench-top glassware systems to modern robotic, plate-based platforms. A central timeline links key milestones in automated chemistry, beginning with the Schlenk line in 1913 and progressing through automated filtration, continuous liquid–liquid extraction, automated distillation, the first rotary evaporator, and semi-automated liquid handling.\n\nOn the left, the older “automate-around-the-bench” approach is shown as a Schlenk-style manifold connected to familiar glass apparatus for filtration, separations, and evaporation. This side suggests automation built by attaching actuators and controls onto traditional laboratory equipment, preserving the geometry of manual workflows.\n\nOn the right, the newer approach replaces bespoke glassware with modular robots and plate-based operations. Small icons indicate pipetting arrays, heating, stirring or shaking, multiwell plates, and linear motion systems, all organized in a standardized format. The arrows and layout imply higher integration, scalability, and programmability compared with the older setup.\n\nOverall, the figure communicates a historical shift in chemistry automation: from adapting manual bench procedures with specialized apparatus to using standardized robotic platforms that miniaturize and coordinate many operations in parallel.",
